:null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":12,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"II,7 92b28-30). Aristotle thinks we do need such \"nominal definitions,\"17 since when we begin","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1066,2234,1116],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0013","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":13,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"looking for X we do not yet know that X exists (and so we cannot yet have more than a nominal","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1124,2226,1174],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0014","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":14,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"definition of X), and yet we must already have some description of X in order to look for X and","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1181,2253,1233],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[{"bbox":null,"end_char":34,"semantic_role":"footnote_marker","span_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0015-visual-qa-marker-00","start_char":32,"styles":["superscript"],"target_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-18","text":"18"}],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0015","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":15,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"to recognize it when we find it.18 But since we will typically \"find\" the object of a science, not by","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1328,2243,1370],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0016","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":16,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"man, although τὸ λευκόν in the sense of the πάθος is ὅπερ λευκόν]. But the point of saying that X is not ὅπερ τόδε","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1376,2180,1418],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0017","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":17,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"τι is that for something to be X (or, for X to be X) is not for it to be ὅπερ τόδε τι. Compare the passage we have","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1423,2230,1465],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0018","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":18,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"cited from Topics III: \"[a good which is] ὅπερ τόδε τι [is better] that one which is not in [the good as] a genus, e.g.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1472,2236,1513],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0019","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":19,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"justice [is better] than the just [man]: for the former is in the good as in a genus, and the latter is not, and the former","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1519,2228,1561],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0020","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":20,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"is ὅπερ good and the latter is not; for nothing is called ὅπερ the genus which is not in the genus, e.g. the white man","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1567,2231,1609],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0021","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":21,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"is not ὅπερ [a] color\" (116a23-7). Here the reason that ὁ δίκαιος is not ὅπερ τόδε τι is for ὁ δίκαιος to be δίκαιος","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1615,2214,1657],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0022","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":22,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"is not for him to be ὅπερ τόδε τι [perhaps Aristotle means \"is not for him to be ὅπερ any τόδε\"; perhaps he means","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1663,2258,1705],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0023","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":23,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"\"is not for him to be ὅπερ the τόδε in question, namely justice, or the good\"] but merely for him to be τοιόνδε (just),","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1711,2232,1753],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0024","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":24,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"or for him to have τόδε (justice) present within him. If X is a τόδε and X is predicated of Y, then it is a sign that, in","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1759,2198,1801],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0025","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":25,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"this predication, Y is not being said to be ὅπερ τόδε if X is predicated of Y paronymously; it is a sign that, in this","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1807,2162,1849],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0026","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":26,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"predication, Y is being said to be ὅπερ τόδε if X is predicated of Y not paronymously [although this sign is not","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1855,2246,1897],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0027","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":27,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"infallible, since X might be predicated of Y homonymously, as Y = σῶμα, X = λευκόν]. In any case, in determining","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1903,2115,1945],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0028","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":28,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"whether ὁ δίκαιος is ὅπερ τόδε τι, the relevant question is how δίκαιος, or δικαιοσύνη, is said of him; and","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1951,2206,1993],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0029","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":29,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"likewise in Z4 with ὁ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος (or ὁ λευκός, for the issue is much the same), the issue is how λευκός or","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,1999,2259,2041],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-native-0030","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf577-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":30,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"λευκόν, or the compound λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος, is said of him. {Perhaps add a note on what I take the point to be of the","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2047,2103,2089],"canoni